  "text": "Sul was a British goddess of healing. She was honored at Bath in southwest England, where hot springs bubble up at about a million gallons of a day. The Romans called the town Aquae Sulis, the waters of Sul. The Roman joined Sul with Minerva, so people often called her Sulis Minerva. The site became a place of commerce and healing. Visitors bathed in the steaming water and prayed for a cure. A bronze figure found in the temple shows Sul as a warrior maiden. More than 6,000 coins were drawn from the spring, most of them Roman. The name of Sul may mean sun and eye, which points to a sun goddess. She also appears in the plural as the Suleviae. Some scholars still looked for a male sun god or read the name as a misspelling of the word for pig. She is sometimes placed beside Brigit, a British war goddess, and the two were joined in the minds of the people.",
